1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,938
Research Computing on Explorer and AICR. Intro to Linux. The shell commands every

2
00:00:04,958 --> 00:00:09,896
cluster user needs: moving around, files, permissions, and the habits that keep a

3
00:00:09,916 --> 00:00:11,040
shared system happy.

4
00:00:11,110 --> 00:00:14,676
Salaam. This is Liam, in for Bear. First, a correction. The terminal isn't an old

5
00:00:14,696 --> 00:00:18,979
way to use a computer. It's the direct way, and on a cluster it's the main one: the

6
00:00:18,999 --> 00:00:22,326
keyboard becomes your only input. This lecture is the basics of the Linux terminal:

7
00:00:22,346 --> 00:00:26,390
what Linux is, how to find your way around the file system, how to make, move and

8
00:00:26,410 --> 00:00:30,455
remove files, how to read them, who may do what to them, two editors, and three tips

9
00:00:30,475 --> 00:00:31,650
that save you every day.

10
00:00:31,720 --> 00:00:35,827
Six terms. The kernel: the core of an operating system, the part that talks to the

11
00:00:35,847 --> 00:00:39,953
hardware. A shell: the program that reads what you type and runs it. A directory: a

12
00:00:39,973 --> 00:00:44,338
folder. A path: the address of a file, written from the root slash or from where you

13
00:00:44,358 --> 00:00:48,207
stand. Root: the administrator account that may do anything. And a flag: a dash and

14
00:00:48,227 --> 00:00:50,270
a letter that changes what a command does.

15
00:00:50,340 --> 00:00:54,555
Linux is a family of operating systems built on the Linux kernel, which Linus

16
00:00:54,575 --> 00:00:59,093
Torvalds created in 1991 in the tradition of Unix, the much older system that also

17
00:00:59,113 --> 00:01:03,329
shaped macOS. It runs on Android phones, on laptops, and on nearly every HPC

18
00:01:03,349 --> 00:01:07,564
cluster. It's free and open source, so anyone can contribute and a huge toolbox

19
00:01:07,584 --> 00:01:12,102
comes at no cost, and it's considered secure and robust, which is why servers and

20
00:01:12,122 --> 00:01:13,010
clusters run it.

21
00:01:13,080 --> 00:01:16,688
Linux has graphical desktops, but underneath is the command line, reached through a

22
00:01:16,708 --> 00:01:20,596
terminal. The prompt tells you four things: your user name, the host you're on,

23
00:01:20,616 --> 00:01:25,620
where you are, and what kind of user you are. A dollar sign means a normal user. A

24
00:01:25,640 --> 00:01:28,969
hash means root, the administrator. A normal user can't install system packages:

25
00:01:28,989 --> 00:01:31,760
ask, and you're told you're not in the sudoers file.

26
00:01:31,830 --> 00:01:36,428
The file system is files and directories, as on Windows or a Mac. The terminal

27
00:01:36,447 --> 00:01:39,814
colours them: blue for directories, green for executables, red for compressed

28
00:01:39,834 --> 00:01:44,123
archives, plain for ordinary files. A path is a file's address. Absolute: from the

29
00:01:44,143 --> 00:01:48,741
root, starting with a slash. Relative: from where you stand. One dot is here. Two

30
00:01:48,761 --> 00:01:50,280
dots is one level up.

31
00:01:50,350 --> 00:01:55,266
Three commands move you around. p w d prints where you are; the tilde is your home.

32
00:01:55,286 --> 00:01:59,622
l s lists what's here. Flags change it: dash l for the long listing with

33
00:01:59,642 --> 00:02:03,106
permissions, owner, size and date; dash a for everything, including hidden files,

34
00:02:03,126 --> 00:02:06,300
whose names start with a dot. Flags combine, dash l a.

35
00:02:06,370 --> 00:02:11,713
c d changes directory. c d into a folder; the prompt shows you're there. c d dot dot

36
00:02:11,733 --> 00:02:16,480
goes one level up. c d alone, or c d tilde, takes you home from anywhere.

37
00:02:16,550 --> 00:02:21,187
Making things. touch creates an empty file. To put text in one, redirect: a single

38
00:02:21,207 --> 00:02:25,533
greater-than overwrites the file, a clean slate; a double one appends to the end.

39
00:02:25,553 --> 00:02:29,880
cat shows the result. And make-dir, m k d i r, makes a directory.

40
00:02:29,950 --> 00:02:34,576
C P copies a file; a directory needs dash capital R, recursive, to copy what's

41
00:02:34,596 --> 00:02:40,150
inside. The original stays. m v moves, and moving a file to a new name is how you

42
00:02:40,170 --> 00:02:40,460
rename.

43
00:02:40,530 --> 00:02:45,536
r m removes a file; dash r removes a directory and everything in it. There is no

44
00:02:45,556 --> 00:02:50,562
trash can and no undo. On a cluster, a removed file is gone, so read the command

45
00:02:50,582 --> 00:02:52,040
twice before you press return.

46
00:02:52,110 --> 00:02:56,928
A symbolic link is a shortcut, like an alias on a Mac. l n dash s, then the target,

47
00:02:56,948 --> 00:03:01,256
then the name you want: point to target, call it link name. In a long listing it

48
00:03:01,276 --> 00:03:05,839
starts with l and shows an arrow to where it points. Now c d my_data jumps to the

49
00:03:05,859 --> 00:03:08,640
deep directory. Move or delete the original and the link breaks.

50
00:03:08,710 --> 00:03:13,038
Reading a file. cat dumps the whole thing into the terminal, fine for a short one.

51
00:03:13,058 --> 00:03:17,658
For a long one use less: a scrollable viewer. Arrows move a line, Page Up and Page

52
00:03:17,678 --> 00:03:22,550
Down a page, Home and End jump to the ends, and q quits. Escape does not close it.

53
00:03:22,620 --> 00:03:27,390
Who may do what. Every file has three audiences: the user who owns it, the group

54
00:03:27,410 --> 00:03:31,282
that owns it, and others, everyone else. And three permissions: read, write, and

55
00:03:31,302 --> 00:03:35,473
execute, which for a directory means entering it. Each permission has a number: read

56
00:03:35,493 --> 00:03:36,970
four, write two, execute one.

57
00:03:37,040 --> 00:03:40,792
Here's a real long-listing line, decoded. The first character is the type: dash for

58
00:03:40,812 --> 00:03:45,372
a file, d for a directory, l for a link. Then nine characters in three groups of

59
00:03:45,392 --> 00:03:49,144
three: the owner's permissions, the group's, everyone else's. r w x: the owner can

60
00:03:49,164 --> 00:03:53,994
read, write and execute. r w dash: the group can read and write but not run. r dash

61
00:03:54,014 --> 00:03:58,035
dash: others can only read. After that: the link count, the owner, the group, the

62
00:03:58,055 --> 00:04:00,460
size in bytes, the modification time, and the name.

63
00:04:00,530 --> 00:04:05,358
Changing permissions with c h mod. By symbol: who, plus or minus, which. g plus x

64
00:04:05,378 --> 00:04:09,904
gives the group execute. u minus x takes it from the owner. Combinations join with

65
00:04:09,924 --> 00:04:14,449
commas. Or by number: three digits, owner, group, others, each the sum of four, two

66
00:04:14,469 --> 00:04:18,692
and one. Seven five one is read-write-execute for the owner, read and execute for

67
00:04:18,712 --> 00:04:20,510
the group, execute only for others.

68
00:04:20,580 --> 00:04:26,322
And ownership with c h own: user colon group, then the file. Dash capital R for a

69
00:04:26,342 --> 00:04:27,000
whole directory.

70
00:04:27,070 --> 00:04:31,684
Two editors live in the terminal. Nano is the simple one: nano and a file name. Its

71
00:04:31,704 --> 00:04:35,499
shortcuts are listed along the bottom, and the caret means Control. Vim is the

72
00:04:35,519 --> 00:04:39,861
powerful one, with two modes. In normal mode you can't type into the file. Press i

73
00:04:39,881 --> 00:04:44,767
for insert mode and type. Press Escape to get back to normal mode. Type colon w q to

74
00:04:44,787 --> 00:04:48,310
save and quit. Stuck? Escape, then colon q exclamation mark quits without saving.

75
00:04:48,380 --> 00:04:53,091
Three tips. Tab completes a long name: type the start, press Tab, the shell fills

76
00:04:53,111 --> 00:04:57,823
the rest. Control C stops a running program, or throws away a line you've mistyped.

77
00:04:57,843 --> 00:04:59,400
And clear wipes the screen.

78
00:04:59,470 --> 00:05:03,797
That's the terminal. Prompt, path, list, move, make, copy, remove with care, read,

79
00:05:03,817 --> 00:05:08,813
permissions, an editor, and Tab. Questions go to the Research Computing team at r c

80
00:05:08,833 --> 00:05:13,160
help at northeastern dot e d u, or to office hours on Zoom.

81
00:05:13,230 --> 00:05:16,980
Let's recap with Claude. The prompt shows your user, host, location, and whether

82
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:22,490
you're root. p w d, l s with flags, and c d move you; absolute paths start from the

83
00:05:22,510 --> 00:05:26,840
root; a single dot means here, two dots mean up. touch, redirection, make-dir, C P

84
00:05:26,860 --> 00:05:32,930
dash R, M V; r m has no undo; l n dash s makes a shortcut. cat for short files, less

85
00:05:32,950 --> 00:05:36,990
for long ones, q quits. Read, write, execute for user, group and others, changed

86
00:05:37,010 --> 00:05:42,500
with c h mod by symbol or by number. Nano is simple; vim is i, Escape, colon w q.

87
00:05:42,520 --> 00:05:44,240
Tab completes, and Control C stops.

88
00:05:44,310 --> 00:05:48,595
Your turn. Paste this into Claude: I'm new to the Linux terminal on a cluster. Give

89
00:05:48,615 --> 00:05:52,363
me a ten-command practice session: make a project folder in my home directory with

90
00:05:52,383 --> 00:05:56,399
two subfolders, create a notes file with one line, append a second line, copy it

91
00:05:56,419 --> 00:06:00,705
into a subfolder, rename the copy, make the file readable by my group but not by

92
00:06:00,725 --> 00:06:05,010
others, and show me the long listing after each step so I can see what changed.

93
00:06:05,030 --> 00:06:08,777
Explain each command in one sentence. Then check two things yourself. Did every path

94
00:06:08,797 --> 00:06:13,890
it wrote start with a slash or a tilde? And did it warn you before the first r m?

95
00:06:13,960 --> 00:06:17,790
Intro to Linux. At Nik Bear Brown.
